{
  "dom_id": "step-q327828",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/f29c8db431035fda7e50771b3baaf6d19f51a60e26582957bbff0f70dbfdc5ed.png)\n\nThe quarter-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$",
  "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/24b56c8addea0d8a0f3effeeefc2788023c452e98b05d10db7893aeebde9ba5e.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$, we get the quarter-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$",
